{"task_id": "HumanEval/93", "solution": "def _is_alphabetic(char_val: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a character is an English alphabet letter.\"\"\"\n    return char_val.isalpha()\ndef _swap_case(char_val: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Swaps the case of an alphabetic character.\"\"\"\n    return char_val.swapcase()\ndef _is_vowel(char_val: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a character is a vowel (a, e, i, o, u, case-insensitive).\"\"\"\n    return char_val.lower() in 'aeiou'\ndef _get_shifted_char(char_val: str, offset: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Returns a character shifted by a given offset in the English alphabet, preserving case and handling wrap-around.\"\"\"\n    start_char_ord = ord('a') if char_val.islower() else ord('A')\n    char_index = ord(char_val) - start_char_ord\n    shifted_index = (char_index + offset) % 26\n    return chr(start_char_ord + shifted_index)\ndef encode(message: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Encodes a message by swapping the case of letters and shifting vowels.\n    Non-alphabetic characters are preserved.\n    \"\"\"\n    encoded_message_chars = []\n    for char in message:\n        if _is_alphabetic(char):\n            char_after_case_swap = _swap_case(char)\n            if _is_vowel(char_after_case_swap):\n                shifted_char = _get_shifted_char(char_after_case_swap, 2)\n                encoded_message_chars.append(shifted_char)\n            else:\n                encoded_message_chars.append(char_after_case_swap)\n        else:\n            encoded_message_chars.append(char)\n    return ''.join(encoded_message_chars)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "def closest_integer(value):\n    '''\n    Create a function that takes a value (string) representing a number\n    and returns the closest integer to it. If the number is equidistant\n    from two integers, round it away from zero.\n\n    Examples\n    >>> closest_integer(\"10\")\n    10\n    >>> closest_integer(\"15.3\")\n    15\n\n    Note:\n    Rounding away from zero means that if the given number is equidistant\n    from two integers, the one you should return is the one that is the\n    farthest from zero. For example closest_integer(\"14.5\") should\n    return 15 and closest_integer(\"-14.5\") should return -15.\n    '''\n    float_value = float(value)\n\n    # Calculate the fractional part to check for .5\n    # Using float_value - int(float_value) to correctly handle negative numbers\n    # int() truncates towards zero.\n    fractional_part = float_value - int(float_value)\n\n    if abs(fractional_part) == 0.5:\n        if float_value >= 0:\n            # For positive X.5, round up (e.g., 14.5 -> 15)\n            return int(float_value) + 1\n        else:\n            # For negative -X.5, round down (e.g., -14.5 -> -15)\n            return int(float_value) - 1\n    else:\n        # For other fractional parts, use standard rounding.\n        # Python's round() for .5 rounds to the nearest even integer,\n        # but our specific .5 cases are handled by the 'if' condition above.\n        return round(float_value)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "import math\ndef closest_integer(value: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Returns the closest integer to a string number, rounding away from zero for equidistant values (X.5).\"\"\"\n    num = float(value)\n\n    # Calculate floor and ceiling integers.\n    floor_val = math.floor(num)\n    ceil_val = math.ceil(num)\n\n    # Check if the number is equidistant (fractional part is 0.5).\n    # Using a small epsilon to handle potential floating-point precision issues.\n    is_equidistant = abs(num - floor_val - 0.5) < 1e-9\n\n    if is_equidistant:\n        if num >= 0:\n            # For positive equidistant numbers (e.g., 14.5), round up.\n            return int(ceil_val)\n        else:\n            # For negative equidistant numbers (e.g., -14.5), round down.\n            return int(floor_val)\n    else:\n        # For non-equidistant numbers, round to the nearest integer.\n        if (num - floor_val) < 0.5:\n            return int(floor_val)\n        else:\n            return int(ceil_val)"}
